        "module": "translation",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[untranslatability]",
        "section": "translation",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Untranslatability and the Limits of Language",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "GREEK: Logos\n  Renderings: \"word,\" \"reason,\" \"discourse,\" \"speech,\" \"argument\"\n  What it means: In different contexts — rational discourse, the\n  divine creative word (John 1:1), logical argument, the rationality\n  of the cosmos. No English word covers all these senses.\n  Consequence: John 1:1 \"In the beginning was the Logos\" becomes\n  \"In the beginning was the Word\" (KJV) — losing the philosophical\n  resonance with Greek rational tradition."
    },
    {
      "id": "06-UNTRANSLATABILITY.md#013",
      "path": [
