        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[untranslatability]",
        "section": "translation",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Untranslatability and the Limits of Language",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "WEAK VERSION (Linguistic Relativity):\n  Language influences the ease, habitualness, and\n  salience of certain cognitive operations — without\n  making others impossible.\n  Status: SUPPORTED by experimental evidence.\n  Examples:\n  • Color perception: speakers with more color terms\n    distinguish colors faster (but can still distinguish\n    colors they have no name for)\n  • Spatial reasoning: languages that use absolute\n    direction (north/south) vs. relative direction\n    (left/right) affect navigation habits\n  • Time perception: Mandarin speakers think of time\n    vertically more often than English speakers"
    },
    {
      "id": "06-UNTRANSLATABILITY.md#021",
      "path": [
        "06-UNTRANSLATABILITY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
