        "current_path": "translation/06-UNTRANSLATABILITY.md",
        "id": "maxim:translation:untranslatability",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "translation",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[untranslatability]",
        "section": "translation",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Untranslatability and the Limits of Language",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "TRANSLATION IMPLICATION:\n  If weak Sapir-Whorf is right: translation across\n  languages with different grammatical/lexical structures\n  is not impossible but involves shifts in cognitive\n  salience. The target-language reader genuinely\n  experiences the translated text differently from\n  a source-language reader — not just due to culture\n  but due to cognitive structure.\n```"
    },
